 "result": "Wrapping up: I've read the relevant code, so here's the answer.\n\n**File:** `src/tessel/views.py`\n\n1. **`VerbView.__init_subclass__`** (line 165): when you subclass `VerbView`, it scans for methods named in `http_method_funcs` (`get`, `post`, …) and sets `cls.methods` to the uppercase set, e.g. `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`.\n\n2. **`View.to_view_func(name, ...)`** (line 86, classmethod on base `View`): builds a plain `view(**kwargs)` closure. On each request it instantiates the class (or reuses one instance if `init_every_request` is `False`) and calls `self.run_view(**kwargs)` via `active_app.ensure_sync`. It then applies `cls.decorators` and attaches `view_class`, `methods`, `provide_automatic_options` and `__name__` to the function.\n\n3. **`VerbView.run_view`** (line 182): does the per-method routing. It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`, falls back to `get` for `HEAD` if there's no handler, and calls it with the URL kwargs (again through `ensure_sync`).\n\n4. **Registration:** `app.attach_route_rule(\"/counter\", view_func=CounterAPI.to_view_func(\"counter\"))`, defined in `src/tessel/sansio/app.py:606`. It reads `view_func.methods` to restrict which HTTP methods the URL rule accepts.",
